1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:11,862
Namo Tassa Bhagavato Harahato Sambha Sambhodasa Homage to the Buddha, the blessed, noble and fully self-awakened one.

2
00:00:13,804 --> 00:00:16,807
So the topic for this month is restraint.

3
00:00:17,888 --> 00:00:20,771
So I've written something, so I'll talk to what I've written.

4
00:00:20,792 --> 00:00:23,355
It's a bit more organized, you know.

5
00:00:24,044 --> 00:00:28,527
So I say the Buddha is very big on restraint, samvara.

6
00:00:29,028 --> 00:00:30,070
And we need to know why.

7
00:00:30,109 --> 00:00:35,595
We need to see how it affects our sense of agency and our self-worth.

8
00:00:37,417 --> 00:00:40,841
A sense of agency means a control, your control over your life.

9
00:00:41,781 --> 00:00:50,109
Now, whenever I reflect on the Dharma and practice, I always go back to the starting point, you know, where all the miseries have their root.

10
00:00:50,651 --> 00:00:51,991
And that, of course, is the self.

11
00:00:52,545 --> 00:00:54,207
So this self is a relationship.

12
00:00:54,307 --> 00:00:56,911
It's a relationship with the world we find ourselves in.

13
00:00:57,390 --> 00:00:59,694
And that world is the world of the six senses.

14
00:00:59,734 --> 00:01:03,518
Remember, the Buddha describes consciousness by the sense base.

15
00:01:03,537 --> 00:01:05,060
Eye consciousness, ear conscious.

16
00:01:05,540 --> 00:01:09,504
And don't forget the mind is also understood as a sense base.

17
00:01:11,608 --> 00:01:14,171
And that's the world we seek happiness in.

18
00:01:14,971 --> 00:01:17,754
Not that there isn't any happiness there.

19
00:01:17,814 --> 00:01:19,436
It's just that we can't maintain it.

20
00:01:19,456 --> 00:01:20,798
We can't trap it.

21
00:01:21,605 --> 00:01:25,468
simply because everything arises and passes away.

22
00:01:25,489 --> 00:01:37,921
So now we know all this, and we know it through our readings and listenings, and we know it through the practice of vipassana, where we try not to get caught up in the fantasies and the stories we tell ourselves.

23
00:01:37,941 --> 00:01:49,992
Now we know that attachments and indulgence to anything we enjoy, from lollipops to intimate relations, will cause some degree of suffering or unsatisfactoriness.

24
00:01:50,444 --> 00:01:51,486
Yet we persist.

25
00:01:51,846 --> 00:01:54,951
We persist because the desires have a hold on us.

26
00:01:55,451 --> 00:02:02,323
And there's often also the fear that we will lose some level of happiness if we let go of what gives us happiness.

27
00:02:03,566 --> 00:02:06,471
So let us first contemplate the loss of agency.

28
00:02:06,510 --> 00:02:09,877
This means we've lost control over some part of our lives.

29
00:02:09,937 --> 00:02:11,759
The desire takes over.

30
00:02:12,296 --> 00:02:19,106
Even when we make strong acts of will, I will not scroll in a moment of inattention.

31
00:02:19,288 --> 00:02:20,669
There we are scrolling.

32
00:02:21,852 --> 00:02:27,419
And even when we're doom scrolling and we know it's cultivating despair, yet we scroll.

33
00:02:28,342 --> 00:02:31,907
So this used to be called a lack of self-discipline.

34
00:02:32,307 --> 00:02:35,111
But these days, nobody wants to hear about discipline.

35
00:02:36,112 --> 00:02:38,456
And it seems to have gone out of our education system.

36
00:02:38,908 --> 00:02:44,016
So when was the last time actually that you ever heard the word discipline, have some self-discipline?

37
00:02:45,399 --> 00:02:54,173
So the common, there is a connotation in the word of punishment, of course, but the original meaning comes from the word learning, a disciple.

38
00:02:54,873 --> 00:02:56,877
And to learn, we need to put our minds to it.

39
00:02:57,177 --> 00:02:59,701
We need to have self-discipline.

40
00:03:01,116 --> 00:03:10,389
So here we have a situation where the ardent meditator knows they need to give up late nights to rise early and practice before setting off to work.

41
00:03:10,409 --> 00:03:16,358
Yet for all the determination, they still get caught up in social media and Netflix.

42
00:03:16,377 --> 00:03:23,889
And what they're actually doing may be wholesome, but it means they miss out on this morning practice, which they give a lot of importance to.

43
00:03:24,269 --> 00:03:25,131
Something has to give.

44
00:03:25,730 --> 00:03:28,254
And that is the role of restraint meditation.

45
00:03:29,331 --> 00:03:37,187
How does it feel when, after all your effort, you don't seem to be able to get rid of a habit that you know is harmful to you and may be harmful to others?

46
00:03:37,948 --> 00:03:39,610
So anger is often a good candidate.

47
00:03:39,711 --> 00:03:45,742
It comes out as sarcasm, spite, outright rage, clever digs, that spike and wound.

48
00:03:45,802 --> 00:03:51,372
Turned in when it beats you up, despises you and hammers home how worthless you are.

49
00:03:51,825 --> 00:04:03,479
So here we need that alert awareness, mindfulness, that senses when the triggers come and not indulge in the rising anger, but actually mentally park it.

50
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:10,086
We're not suppressing it, but to put it to one side and then raise goodwill, lay over it.

51
00:04:11,609 --> 00:04:18,456
When we turn inwards afterwards at some time, you know, we can embrace it with a sense of care and just allow it to manifest.

52
00:04:19,559 --> 00:04:21,680
So some part of the heart is diseased.

53
00:04:23,045 --> 00:04:25,708
Now, here's the Buddha's description of right effort.

54
00:04:26,889 --> 00:04:38,903
It's when a mendicant generates enthusiasm, tries, makes an effort, exerts the mind and strives so that immoral, unskillful qualities don't arise.

55
00:04:39,944 --> 00:04:50,637
They generate enthusiasm, try, make an effort, exert the mind and strive so that immoral, unskillful qualities that have arisen are given up.

56
00:04:51,478 --> 00:04:54,747
That's the bad side.

57
00:04:54,767 --> 00:04:56,973
The good side is obviously balancing it.

58
00:04:57,033 --> 00:05:07,259
So they generate enthusiasm, try, make an effort, exert the mind and strive so that skillful qualities that have not arisen do so.

59
00:05:08,134 --> 00:05:23,913
and they generate enthusiasm, try, make an effort, exert the mind and strive so that skillful qualities that have arisen are not lost, but increase, mature and are fulfilled by development.

60
00:05:24,473 --> 00:05:26,735
This is called right effort.

61
00:05:28,598 --> 00:05:32,843
Now, you'll also see that in the discourse on how to establish mindfulness.

62
00:05:33,817 --> 00:05:42,870
So here we have four ways which the Buddha asks us to exercise through which we get the purpose for our restraint.

63
00:05:43,550 --> 00:05:47,415
So the first is, of course, the way of morality and ethics.

64
00:05:47,435 --> 00:05:56,307
If what we're doing is harmful, then obviously we should restrain ourselves, harmful to ourselves or others or both ourselves and others.

65
00:05:56,327 --> 00:06:01,935
It may be that someone has decided to become a vegetarian or vegan on moral rather than health grounds.

66
00:06:02,387 --> 00:06:05,411
So now they have a connection with the suffering of farm animals.

67
00:06:06,052 --> 00:06:12,160
Because of past habit, they may find themselves buying fish and chips, that iconic British meal.

68
00:06:12,940 --> 00:06:17,466
Now, fish often don't quite make it into the definition of sentient beings.

69
00:06:18,226 --> 00:06:24,675
As a monk in Sri Lanka once quipped, fish are considered self-propelling animated vegetable.

70
00:06:26,156 --> 00:06:31,803
I've heard people say they can't feel pain because they're cold, such as when our fingers freeze, we don't feel anything.

71
00:06:32,812 --> 00:06:43,430
So accepting that fish are sentient beings, that person may feel guilty while they're eating, and they avoid the feeling by eating more quickly, stuffing themselves.

72
00:06:44,853 --> 00:06:47,338
Now, the second restraint is by way of mindfulness.

73
00:06:47,398 --> 00:06:55,310
So while eating the fish, such a person who's practiced vipassana will become more acutely aware of any feelings of guilt or remorse.

74
00:06:55,915 --> 00:06:58,237
So much so, it almost spoils the pleasure.

75
00:06:58,737 --> 00:07:03,163
This is where guilt has become a guardian to warners of such behavior.

76
00:07:03,882 --> 00:07:10,670
But where there's unskillful action, there will also follow unwholesome consequences.

77
00:07:12,372 --> 00:07:16,096
Allied to this is the awareness of the sixth sense bases.

78
00:07:16,757 --> 00:07:23,644
In hot weather, the shapely bodies of women and men come into view, and upon seeing, erotic feelings may arise.

79
00:07:24,502 --> 00:07:28,107
This is natural in the sense that it has a biological conditioning.

80
00:07:28,127 --> 00:07:34,576
But then do we turn away and refuse to indulge or do we linger and develop lust?

81
00:07:35,598 --> 00:07:47,975
Lust can become obsessive, but even at a low level, left ungratified, lust leaves a feeling of discomfort and then a discomposure, a feeling that consequence encourages.

82
00:07:48,055 --> 00:07:53,343
That feeling, recognizing it, encourages us to restrain ourselves.

83
00:07:55,449 --> 00:08:03,360
Such practice is a direct way of understanding the second noble truth, the cause of suffering.

84
00:08:03,761 --> 00:08:06,444
The noble truth of the cause of suffering, O monks, is this.

85
00:08:06,725 --> 00:08:16,538
It is the craving which produces re-becoming, rebirth accompanied by passionate greed and finding fresh delight now here, now there.

86
00:08:16,939 --> 00:08:24,629
Namely, craving for sense pleasures, craving for becoming, craving for non-becoming, not wanting stuff.

87
00:08:25,774 --> 00:08:42,495
And it fulfills one of the right intentions, I prefer the right attitudes, of the second step of the Noble Eightfold Path, namely renunciation, which is gently pulling ourselves away from indulgence and attachments.

88
00:08:42,515 --> 00:08:46,922
And you could argue that renunciation is the whole of the spiritual path.

89
00:08:48,455 --> 00:09:01,628
Finally, it is the understanding of the direct experience in our sitting and ordinary daily life of the unwholesome qualities of indulgence and attachment that develops the wisdom to practice restraint.

90
00:09:02,528 --> 00:09:11,398
So, samvara is the spiritual exercise of refraining from fulfilling desires that we perceive to be harmful.

91
00:09:12,259 --> 00:09:14,360
There's a holding back of that energy.

92
00:09:14,864 --> 00:09:24,193
But as always, if done with annoyance or self-hatred, self-accusation, that would be just suppressing it, not transforming it.

93
00:09:24,414 --> 00:09:30,061
Moreover, any annoyance and self-hatred will simply reinforce those attitudes.

94
00:09:30,400 --> 00:09:38,289
Instead, we wait for it to pass, as when we wait to cross the road, waiting for cars to pass.

95
00:09:38,741 --> 00:09:44,347
So this allows the fuel of the desire to be expended, the habit to be weakened.

96
00:09:44,749 --> 00:09:48,913
And the more we do this, just like any exercise, it becomes easier.

97
00:09:50,796 --> 00:09:56,962
The more we resist temptations, the better we feel about ourselves, simply because we've taken back control.

98
00:09:57,903 --> 00:10:06,133
The more we feel in charge of our lives, the more we follow the ways of skillful actions, the more we feel happy about ourselves.

99
00:10:06,669 --> 00:10:12,638
The more we feel satisfied and contented with ourselves, the more our self-worth grows.

100
00:10:13,057 --> 00:10:20,207
And self-worth is that deep inner feeling and knowing of one's own goodness.

101
00:10:20,227 --> 00:10:23,893
Now, one way to develop strong resolution is to fast.

102
00:10:23,913 --> 00:10:32,923
So you'll find some kind of fasting in most religions, such as Lent for Christians and Ramadan for Muslims.

103
00:10:32,943 --> 00:10:35,648
In the Buddhist teaching, it's included when a layperson

104
00:10:36,067 --> 00:10:39,812
takes the eight precepts and they undertake not to eat after midday.

105
00:10:41,293 --> 00:10:52,470
Now, this is not to be confused with dieting for health's sake or fasting as a practice of self-mortification, sort of beating oneself up, but to become aware of any attachment we have to eating and drinking.

106
00:10:53,110 --> 00:11:01,702
Particularly feelings of hunger are one thing, but the inability to stop ourselves from immediately reacting, hence constant snacking, is another.

107
00:11:02,071 --> 00:11:06,999
So in my own case, giving up a cup of tea in the morning requires total commitment.

108
00:11:08,881 --> 00:11:12,828
Now, there's also a practice that was developed by St. Augustine.

109
00:11:13,288 --> 00:11:24,548
In his autobiography, he tells us that as a young man, he had difficulty restraining his lust and prayed, give me chastity and continence, O Lord, but not yet.

110
00:11:26,270 --> 00:11:27,673
So that's an honest confession.

111
00:11:28,260 --> 00:11:33,667
Now, there are four parts to this, and some of you who have been on retreat with me will know them.

112
00:11:33,687 --> 00:11:35,989
So it's can, ought, want, and will.

113
00:11:38,491 --> 00:11:45,259
Now, we might sometimes fool ourselves and say that we cannot give up tea and coffee.

114
00:11:46,020 --> 00:11:49,043
But when we look a bit closer, there's usually a won't.

115
00:11:49,384 --> 00:11:53,668
So the first step is to speak gently into our hearts, directly into our hearts.

116
00:11:53,749 --> 00:11:54,690
It's I can, you know.

117
00:11:55,042 --> 00:12:00,025
I can refrain from drinking too much tea, et cetera.

118
00:12:00,046 --> 00:12:03,369
And I can add for my own benefit and the benefit of others.

119
00:12:06,292 --> 00:12:08,494
And you've got to wait.

120
00:12:08,793 --> 00:12:09,955
You've got to keep repeating that.

121
00:12:09,975 --> 00:12:15,879
You say, I can, I can, I can do it until, especially if there's a wonk in the way, right?

122
00:12:15,960 --> 00:12:18,182
So you keep saying that until you feel it's digested.

123
00:12:18,241 --> 00:12:24,086
And there is definitely a knowing that one can give up tea, right?

124
00:12:25,214 --> 00:12:27,596
And then we have to accept full responsibility.

125
00:12:27,716 --> 00:12:28,498
That's the next step.

126
00:12:28,518 --> 00:12:29,158
I ought to.

127
00:12:29,740 --> 00:12:31,741
My own benefit and the benefits of others.

128
00:12:32,001 --> 00:12:32,283
Yeah.

129
00:12:33,264 --> 00:12:40,072
And then once that's taken on, again, you have to feel a bit of digestion there, a bit of acceptance.

130
00:12:40,192 --> 00:12:44,336
Once that's done, it's normally not so difficult to get that want.

131
00:12:44,476 --> 00:12:46,078
Yeah, I want to do it.

132
00:12:46,299 --> 00:12:48,001
You know, this is what I really want to do.

133
00:12:48,062 --> 00:12:50,664
It's my own benefit, benefit of others.

134
00:12:51,150 --> 00:12:54,797
And then finally, of course, that strong, determined act of will.

135
00:12:55,136 --> 00:12:55,638
So I will.

136
00:12:56,058 --> 00:12:56,919
I will do it.

137
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,624
You see, when the occasion arises, will.

138
00:13:02,469 --> 00:13:06,053
And of course, you can practice this with virtue as well, you see.

139
00:13:06,215 --> 00:13:08,697
So can't or won't will.

140
00:13:09,019 --> 00:13:12,203
So now I personally find this best done right in the morning.

141
00:13:12,625 --> 00:13:16,429
And if I want to get up when the bell goes, I do it before I go to sleep.

142
00:13:16,870 --> 00:13:19,335
I can get up when the alarm goes.

143
00:13:20,902 --> 00:13:25,429
I think you'll find it really effective in everyday practice.

144
00:13:25,450 --> 00:13:33,465
So what I'd like to do now is to actually do this practice and then just slide into the meditation.

145
00:13:34,166 --> 00:13:37,812
So if you find yourself in a nice meditative posture,

146
00:13:48,120 --> 00:13:56,408
So you can either say I can refrain from or I resist the temptation to whatever works for you, right?

147
00:13:56,488 --> 00:14:08,758
Now what you've got to choose is some habit that you have which you'd like to be rid of or at least let it have a less hold on you.

148
00:14:08,979 --> 00:14:11,221
That's something like that, yeah.

149
00:14:11,240 --> 00:14:17,706
So if you choose that sort of habit or attitude, yeah,

150
00:14:19,491 --> 00:14:35,255
And then once you've chosen it, so you just very gently repeat into your heart, you see, I can, I can refrain from, and it's for my own benefit and the benefit of others.

151
00:15:10,370 --> 00:15:15,900
So when you're doing this by yourself, you can also obviously take a bit more time if this isn't enough time.

152
00:15:16,402 --> 00:15:21,933
But once you can get that feeling of you can, you can resist the temptation.

153
00:15:22,875 --> 00:15:24,038
Then we go on to the next one.

154
00:15:24,097 --> 00:15:24,599
I ought to.

155
00:15:24,840 --> 00:15:28,868
I ought to do it for my own benefit and the benefit of others.

156
00:15:58,294 --> 00:16:07,186
Once that's been developed within us to a point where we really take on that responsibility, then we can move into cajoling the heart.

157
00:16:07,206 --> 00:16:07,948
Yes, I want to.

158
00:16:08,289 --> 00:16:09,333
I want to refrain.

159
00:16:35,216 --> 00:16:42,296
And as you feel that that now is really built up, we then make that act of will.

160
00:16:42,596 --> 00:16:44,140
I will refrain.

161
00:16:45,705 --> 00:16:51,100
You can add when the situation comes, it doesn't really matter, but just getting that commitment.

162
00:17:20,492 --> 00:17:33,682
And although this talk has been about refraining from something which is unwholesome, we can, just to finish off, use these in order to develop a virtue.

163
00:17:33,961 --> 00:17:38,551
So in the same way, we develop a virtue that we'd like to develop more.

164
00:17:47,070 --> 00:17:52,396
So I can develop this virtue for my own benefit and the benefit of others.

165
00:18:15,402 --> 00:18:16,182
I ought to.

166
00:18:46,116 --> 00:18:47,015
I want to.

167
00:19:14,567 --> 00:19:16,189
And finally I will.

168
00:19:16,229 --> 00:19:43,713
And so we can begin our period of meditation.

